iOS native views: GPUI's view in a host view, and a rotation that turns GPUI's content, holes and views as one (needs 0062 and 0061).

What:
- Rotation: GPUI's view and the containers live in a host view inside the view controller's plain root view. In
  `handle_layout_change` (inside UIKit's rotation animation) the host takes the new size without animation and only
  its centre follows the turn, and the frame at the new size is drawn right there, in the same transaction, so GPUI's
  content, the holes and the views turn as one rigid picture instead of GPUI's last frame stretching while the views
  jump. `viewWillTransitionToSize:withTransitionCoordinator:` marks the turn (`Rotation::Turning` until the
  coordinator's completion), during which the holes are cut a pixel inside the views (core's `hole_inset`, through
  0061's `set_native_view_hole_inset`), since Core Animation resamples the turning edges; the frame after the turn
  cuts them exactly again. Display Zoom (`nativeScale` != `scale`) gets the same inset.

Where: new files behind one-line hooks in upstream's window.rs and ios.rs: ios/window/rotation.rs (the nesting, the
turn, the inset, the resized frame) and ios/native_view_state.rs (`Rotation` as a pure transition, table-tested on
the host by scripts/patch-test.sh, `ios-pure`; 0064 adds `KeyboardInput`). 0062's native_views.rs comments now
name the host view, where its containers and colour go.

Why the nesting is always there, not only while views show: GPUI's CAMetalLayer is its view's own backing layer, so a
native view can only go beneath it as a sibling in a superview, and upstream makes GPUI's view the view controller's
root. Creating the nesting for the first view would swap the view controller's view and move GPUI's view, with its
text input view (the first responder while a GPUI field has the keyboard), into new superviews of a live window
each time the first view comes or the last goes. Two plain views that draw nothing cost no frame; every app gets the
rigid turn.

Why: Flutter's FlutterViewController.mm (`viewWillTransitionToSize:withTransitionCoordinator:`) only delays its
viewport update to halfway through a turn, which halves the stretch; rendering the new size at once keeps the views
in step instead.

Tests: `Rotation::resamples`'s table (settled, turning, Display Zoom). Checked (0062–c together) on the iOS
simulator as 0062 says, also through a rotation to landscape and back.

Upstream PR: not filed (filing paused, patch rule 14).

